    body: [
  "INTRODUCTION TO EARLY \nMEDIEVAL INDIA (C.750–1000 CE)\nThe period between 750 and 1200 AD is referred to as an \nearly medieval period of Indian History. It was earlier treated \nby historians as a ‘dark phase’. It was so because during this \ntime the whole country was divided into numerous regional \nstates which were busy fighting with each other. Following \nHarsha’s demise, the region experienced fragmentation, \nwith numerous states engaged in constant conflicts, leading \nto frequent alterations in their borders. Prominent kingdoms \nduring this period included Kashmir, Gandhara, Sind, \nGujarat, Kanauj, Ajmer, Malwa, Bengal, and Assam. In the \nearly eighth century, Kashmir held sway, but later, the Palas \nof Bengal emerged as dominant rulers until the ascendancy \nof the Pratiharas as the most influential leaders in Northern \nIndia. However, in the tenth century, the Rashtrakutas from \nthe Deccan sought to expand their influence into Northern \nIndia, ultimately encountering failure in their endeavour.\nThe Gurjara-Pratiharas, the earliest among the Rajput rulers, \nwere led by their first significant leader, Harishchandra. \nHarishchandra expanded his influence across Rajputana. The \nGurjaras had distinct branches, with one ruling in Gujarat\nand another in Avanti. Engaging in a three-cornered contest, \nthe Pratiharas found themselves contending with the Palas \nof Bengal and the Rashtrakutas of Deccan. Over time, the \nPratiharas experienced a decline in strength.\nThe Chauhans, regarded as the most courageous among the \nRajput clans, held sway in Ajmer. Vigraharaj, their prominent \nking, occupied even Delhi. Consequently, the Chauhans\nconfronted the Muslim onslaught led by Muhammad of \nGhori. Alongside the Chauhans, the Paramaras emerged \nas significant Rajput rulers during this era. Notably, Bhoja, \ntheir most prominent king, left a lasting impact through both \nmilitary conquests and notable cultural contributions in the \nannals of Rajput history.\nThe Tripartite Struggle\nThe period between 750 and 1000 CE in India was a time of \ndynamic political evolution, marked by the growth of three \npivotal powers: the Gurjara-Pratiharas in Western India and \nthe upper Gangetic valley, the Palas in Eastern India, and the \nRashtrakutas in the Deccan. These dynasties were engaged \nin a protracted conflict known as the ‘tripartite struggle’, \nprimarily for control over the resource-rich Gangetic \nplains. Despite their rivalries, these empires fostered stable \nconditions, expanded agriculture, and were patrons of art \nand architecture, particularly in temple building.\nFig. 1.1: The Tripartite Struggle\nThe \nTripartite \nStruggle\nGurjara-Pratiharas in Western \nIndia and the upper Gangetic valley\nPalas in Eastern India\nRashtrakutas in the Deccan\nTHE PRATIHARA DYNASTY\nOrigins and Significance\nThe Gurjara-Pratiharas, also known as Pratiharas, originated \nfrom the Gurjaratra or South Western Rajasthan, who were \npastoralists and warriors. The dynasty drew inspiration from \nthe epic hero Lakshmana, Rama’s door-keeper, leading to their \ntitle “Pratihara” (door-keeper). Renowned for their sculpture \nand temple architecture, the greatest development of their \narchitectural style is evident at Khajuraho, a UNESCO World \nHeritage Site. The dynasty was established by Harichandra in \nthe region of Jodhpur, South-western Rajasthan, and rose to \nprominence during Nagabhata I’s reign.\nDistinguished Monarchs and their Reigns \nNagabhata I (c. 730–760 CE)\n Defending Against Arab Invasions:\n• Resistance to Arab Forces: Nagabhata I is celebrated \nfor his successful resistance against Arab invasions, \na key event in Indian history. He notably defeated \nthe Arab army under Junaid and Tamin during the \nCaliphate campaigns, marking a significant chapter \nin the defence of the Indian sub-continent against \nexternal invasions.\n• Territorial Control: He exercised control over strategic \nregions like Malwa, Rajputana, and Gujarat, areas \ncrucial for the stability and expansion of the Pratihara \nempire.\n Military Challenges:\n• Conflict with Rashtrakutas: Despite his victories \nagainst the Arabs, Nagabhata I faced defeat at the \nhands of the Rashtrakuta king, Dhruva. This defeat \nunderscored the competitive and challenging nature \nof Indian politics during this period.\nThe Early Medieval India 1"
]
