  "2 Medieval India \nFig. 1.2: India during 900 CE.\nVatsaraja (c. 780–800 CE)\n Expansion and the Tripartite Struggle:\n• North Indian Expansion: Vatsaraja, one of the \nsuccessors of Nagabhata I, significantly extended the \nPratihara rule over a large part of North India. His \ndecision to make Kannauj, in Western Uttar Pradesh, \nthe capital was a strategic one, given the city’s \nhistorical and cultural significance.\n• Conflict with Contemporary Powers: Vatsaraja’s \nexpansionist policies brought him into direct conflict \nwith Dharamapala, the Pala King of Bengal and Bihar, \nand again with the Rashtrakutas under Dhruva. This \nmarked the beginning of what is historically known \nas the ‘tripartite struggle,’ a prolonged conflict \ninvolving the Pratiharas, Palas, and Rashtrakutas for \nsupremacy over the Northern Indian plains.\n• Endurance of Pratihara for hold over Kannauj:\nDespite the ongoing struggles and shifting power \ndynamics, the Pratiharas maintained their hold over \nKannauj, a testament to their resilience and strategic \nacumen.\n Geopolitical Significance:\n• The reigns of Nagabhata I and Vatsaraja highlight \nthe geopolitical complexities of Early Medieval India. \nThe period was marked by a blend of defensive \nand expansionist strategies, with rulers needing to \nprotect their realms from external invasions while \nalso engaging in territorial expansion.\n Cultural and Administrative Impact:\n• Kannauj as a Cultural Hub: The establishment of \nKannauj as the capital under Vatsaraja significantly \ncontributed to its development as a cultural and \npolitical centre in Northern India.\n• Military and Diplomatic Manoeuvring: The ability \nof these rulers to navigate the challenging political \nlandscape through military and diplomatic means \nillustrates their capabilities in statecraft.\nFig. 1.3: Timeline of the Pratihara Rulers\nThe Rulers of Pratihara Dynasty\nVatsaraja (c. 780-800 CE)\nMahipala I (c. 913-944 CE)\nNagabhata I (c. 730-760 CE)\nMahendrapala I (c. 885-910 CE)\nNagabhata II (c. 800-833 CE)\nRajyapala (c. 960-1018 CE)\nMihira Bhoja/Bhoja I (c.836-885 CE)\nYashpala (c. 1024-1036)"
