    subtitle: "संपूर्ण अभ्यास नोंद",
    body: [
  "The Early Medieval India 5\n Territorial and Cultural Influence:\n• Extent of the Kingdom: The Pala kingdom \nencompassed Bengal and Bihar, including major cities \nlike Pataliputra, Vikrampura, Ramvati (Varendra), \nMonghyr (Munger), Tamralipti, and Jaggadala.\n• Tripartite Struggle: The Palas, along with the \nRashtrakutas and Pratiharas, were engaged in \nthe prolonged tripartite struggle, with Bihar and \nmodern east Uttar Pradesh being the primary areas \nof contention. Despite this, the Palas predominantly \ncontrolled Bihar and Bengal.\n• Buddhist Patronage: The Pala kings, devout \nfollowers of Mahayana and Tantric Buddhism, \nactively promoted this religion. They built numerous \nmonasteries and temples, leaving a lasting legacy in \nTibetan Buddhism.\nDistinguished Monarchs and their Reigns\nGopala (around c.750 CE)\n Founding and Expansion:\n• Election to Kingship: Gopala was democratically \nelected by the notable men of the realm to rescue the \npeople from ‘Matsya-Nyaya’ (a period of anarchy), \nas noted in the Khalimpur Copper Plate Inscription\nof Dharmapala.\n• Conquests: He displaced the later Guptas of \nMagadha and the Khadga dynasty of Eastern Bengal, \nconsolidating his rule.\n• Buddhist Patronage: An ardent Buddhist, Gopala \nbuilt the famous Odantapuri Monastery, as per the \naccounts of the Buddhist scholar Taranatha. \nDharmapala (c.770−810 CE)\n Military and Political Achievements:\n• Early Setbacks and Recovery: Initially defeated by \nthe Pratiharas and Rashtrakutas, Dharmapala later \nconquered large parts of Northern India, elevating the \nPala empire. He was again defeated by Nagabhatta II \nnear Munger.\n• Kannauj Durbar: He held a durbar at Kannauj, \nattended by numerous vassal chiefs. Here, he \ninstalled his puppet ruler, Chakrayudha, effectively \nmaking Kannauj a dependency while directly ruling \nBengal and Bihar.\n• Widespread Influence: Dharmapala’s sovereignty \nwas acknowledged by rulers across various regions, \nincluding Punjab, the Western hill states, Rajputana, \nMalwa, and Berar.\n Cultural and Educational Contributions:\n• Vikramshila Monastery: He found the Vikramshila \nMonastery near Bhagalpur, Bihar, a centre of learning \nakin to Nalanda University, attracting students from \nacross India and Tibet. He was responsible for the \ntranslation of many Sanskrit texts into Tibetan.\n• Somapuri Monastery: He established the Somapuri \nmonastery and supported Nalanda University with grants.\n• Santarakshita’s Role: During his reign, the Buddhist \nscholar Santarakshita, an abbot (head of the monks) \nof Nalanda, founded the Yogacara-Svatantrika￾Madhyamika philosophical school and played a \npivotal role in introducing Buddhism to Tibet.\nThe Pala period was marked by significant developments \nin religious and educational spheres. Their patronage \nof Buddhism and educational institutions like Nalanda \nand Vikramshila played a crucial role in the cultural and \nintellectual development of the region. This period also saw \na synthesis of various philosophical traditions under scholars \nlike Santarakshita, which contributed to a rich intellectual \ntradition.\nDevapala (c. 810−850 CE)\n Territorial Expansion and Military Achievements:\n• Expansion of the Empire: Devapala significantly \nextended the Pala empire to include Pragjyotishpur/\nKamarupa (Assam), parts of Orissa (Utkala), and \nmodern Nepal. He claimed dominion over a vast \nexpanse, from the Himalayas to the Vindhyas and \nfrom the Eastern to the Western oceans.\n• Victories and Conquests: His inscriptions record \nvictories over various groups, including the Hunas, \nthe lords of Gurjaras (likely Mihir Bhoja), and the \nDravidas, indicating a period of extensive military \nactivity and expansion.\n Patronage and Diplomatic Relations:\n• Support for Buddhism: Devapala was a staunch \npatron of Buddhism, continuing the Pala dynasty’s \ntradition. His support extended to the renowned \nNalanda Monastery.\n• International Diplomacy: A notable diplomatic \nepisode involved the Sailendra dynasty king \nBalaputradeva, the ruler of Suvarnadvipa (Indonesian \nArchipelago). \n Balaputradeva requested Devapala to grant five \nvillages to Nalanda Monastery, which Devapala \nfulfilled, appointing Viradeva as the head of the \nmonastery.\n• Court Poet: Vajradatta, a Buddhist scholar and Devapala’s \ncourt poet authored the ‘Lokesvarasataka’, reflecting the \nintellectual and religious vibrancy of his court.\n Recognition and Challenges:\n• Arab Merchant’s Account: The Pala empire during \nDevapala’s reign was referred to as “Ruhimi or Ruhma \nDharma” by the Arab merchant Sulaiman. Sulaiman \nnoted that Devapala was engaged in warfare with the \nPratiharas and Rashtrakutas and had a formidable \ntroop strength.\n• Decline of Power: Towards the late 9th century, the \npower of the Palas began to wane. The struggle for \ncontrol of Kannauj with the Gurjara-Pratiharas and \nRashtrakutas did not favour the Palas. Additionally, \nregions like Assam and Orissa began to assert their \nindependence."
]
